 @cunnyman  Yes, but I bet you're playing on Ultra, right? XB1X games are usually somewhere between medium to high, sometimes very high, but not ultra, in equivalent visual quality settings.

But, yeah, undoubtedly consoles are better optimised; having only 1 or 2 specifications to specialise the game design & implementation to will pretty much always help! When you compare this to the thousands of potential hardware variations on PC, it's not very hard to see why!)

I just believe that the difference these days is considerably smaller than it once was, since consoles now run a non-trivial OS, share the same overall hardware design architecture and parts, and so on.

 @hrvstrr  Did you not watch this video? All performance benchmark DF got were from games we have now. Crysis 3, Far Cry 5, Shadow of the Tomb Raider, Ghost Recon etc. Their benchmark indicates that for the exact same launch price of the GTX 1080 ti, the RTX 2080 is a more capable card. The benchmark in this video clearly shows this. 

With 2080 you have a more capable card that isn't even the "ti" variant of the RTX series, outperforming the "ti" variant of the GTX series. It doesn't even end there because apart from being a more powerful card for the same price as the GTX 1080ti when it launched, the RTX line of cards have modern feature sets not remotely feasible on Pascal architecture. 

Were not even talking about the "RTX 2080 ti" that cost $999. At face value that may look expensive until you realise the RTX 2080 ti outperforms the $3000 Titan V.

